To add IDs to HTML elements in the provided code, you can follow these steps:

1. First, let's create a function `getId` that will generate unique IDs for each element based on its position in the array. This will ensure that no two elements have the same ID.

   ```jsx
   const getId = (index) => `element-${index}`;
   ```

2. Next, we'll modify the JSX for the `CreateObjectView` component to pass the index of each element as a prop and use it to generate an ID. We'll also add the generated ID to the element itself.

   ```jsx
   export const CreateObjectView = observer(({ model, index }) => {
     const id = getId(index);

     return (
       <div className="Asset" id={id}>
         {model.availableAssets[index]}

         <button onClick={() => {
           if (model.selectedAsset === model.availableAssets[index]) {
             model.setSelectedAsset(null);
           } else {
             model.setSelectedAsset(model.availableAssets[index]);
           }
         }} disabled={model.selectedAsset === null || model.selectedAsset === model.availableAssets[index]}>
           {model.selectedAsset === model.availableAssets[index] ? "Remove selection" : "Select asset"}
         </button>

         <button onClick={() => {
           model.createObject(model.availableAssets[index]).then(() => {
             // do something after creating object
           });
           model.setSelectedAsset(null);
         }} disabled={model.selectedAsset !== model.availableAssets[index]}>
           Add object
         </button>

         <button onClick={() => {
           model.cleanupScene();
         }} disabled={model.availableAssets.length <= 1}>
           Cleanup scene
         </button>
       </div>
     );
   });
   ```

3. Finally, you can use these IDs to select or manipulate specific elements using JavaScript or CSS. For example:

