To make the circle element look more 3D, you can apply the following CSS properties:

1. Perspective: This property adds a 3D effect by creating a virtual point in space where parallel lines appear to meet. You can set this on the parent container of the circle element, for example:

```css
#chart-area {
  perspective: 800px;
}
```

2. Transform: This property rotates or scales an element in 3D space. You can add rotation along the X, Y, and Z axis to create a 3D effect. For example:

```css
.circle {
  transform: rotateX(10deg) rotateY(10deg) translateZ(100px);
}
```

This will rotate the circle around the X and Y axis and move it forward by 100 pixels along the Z axis.

3. Box-shadow: This property adds a shadow to the element that gives it depth. Here's an example:

```css
.circle {
  box-shadow: 10px 10px 20px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.75), inset 0px 0px 5px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.75);
}
```

The first box-shadow creates an outer shadow, while the second one (with the `inset` keyword) creates an inner shadow that makes the element pop out.

4. Background: This property sets the background color of the element. Here's an example that uses a radial gradient:

```css
.circle {
  background: radial-gradient(white, lightblue);
}
```

5. Filter: This property applies special effects to elements. In this case, we're using the `drop-shadow()` function to add a subtle drop shadow to the circle.

By combining these CSS properties, you can create a convincing 3D effect for your circle element. Remember to test it in different browsers as support for 3D CSS may vary between them.

