To fix the CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) error in WordPress when using Next.js, you can follow these steps:

1. Install the necessary packages:

```bash
npm install --save cross-origin-resource-sharing cors middleware
```

2. Create a new file called `middleware.js` in your project's root directory and add the following code:

```javascript
const CORE_OPTIONS = {
  methods: ['GET', 'HEAD'], // Allow only GET and HEAD requests
};

const cors = require('cors');
const core = require('cross-origin-resource-sharing');

module.exports = (req, res, next) => {
  core(req, res, CORE_OPTIONS);
  cors()(req, res, next);
};
```

3. Update your `pages/_app.js` file to include the new middleware:

```javascript
import App from 'next/app';
import withReduxStore from '../lib/with-redux-store';
import middleware from '../middleware';

class MyApp extends App {
  // ...
}

MyApp.getInitialProps = async (appContext) => {
  // ...
}

export default withReduxStore(middleware)(MyApp);
```

This will ensure that CORS headers are set on all requests made by Next.js to your WordPress site, which should fix the error. You may need to adjust the `CORE_OPTIONS` object to suit your specific requirements.

To fix the CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) error in WordPress when using Next.js, you can follow these steps:

1. Install the `cors` package in your Next.js project by running `npm install --save cors` or `yarn add -D cors`.

2. Import the `cors` module at the top of your server file (usually `pages/api/[...next].js`) and use it to enable CORS for all requests:

```javascript
import cors from 'cors';
import next from 'next';
import app from '../config/next.config.js';
import connectDB from '../db/connect.js';
import { parse } from 'querystring';

const dev = process.env.NODE_ENV !== 'production';
const startServer = async () => {
    await connectDB();

    const server = next({ dev });
    const handle = server.getRequestHandler();

    server.prepare().then(() => {
        // Enable CORS for all requests
        const corsOptions = {
            origin: (origin, callback) => {
                callback(null, true);
            },
        };

        server.setMiddleware([cors(corsOptions), parse]);

        const port = process.env.PORT || 3000;
        server.listen(port, (err) => {
            if (err) throw err;
            console.log(`Next.js server ready on http://localhost:${port}`);
        });

        // Handle non-Next.js requests
        const handler = server.getRequestHandler();
        handler(req, res);
    });
};

startServer();
```

In this example, we're importing the `cors` module and creating a new CORS middleware with an origin verification function that allows all origins. We're also parsing query strings and setting up Next.js server middleware.

3. Save the changes and restart your server to test the CORS error fix. You should be able to make cross-domain requests without any issues now!

