To fix the CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) error in WordPress while using Next.js, you can follow these steps:

1. Install the `cors` package in your Next.js project by running the following command in your terminal:

```bash
npm install cors
```

2. In your `server/pages/_api/wp-json.js` file, add the following code to handle the CORS issue:

```javascript
const next = require('next');
const app = next({ dev: process.env.NODE_ENV !== 'production' });
const handle = app.getRequestHandler();
const cors = require('cors');
const origin = new URL(process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_VERCEL_URL).origin;

app.prepare().then(() => {
  const server = app.getServer();

  server.listen(3000, (err) => {
    if (err) throw err;
    console.log(`> Ready on https://${origin}`);
  });

  server.get('/', cors(), (req, res) => {
    return handle(req, res);
  });
});

export const config = {
  api: {
    bodyParser: false,
  },
};
```

3. Add the following code to your `.env` file to set the `NEXT_PUBLIC_VERCEL_URL` environment variable:

```bash
NEXT_PUBLIC_VERCEL_URL=https://your-vercel-url.com
```

This will enable CORS for requests made from any domain. If you want to restrict it to specific domains, replace `new URL(process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_VERCEL_URL).origin` with the list of allowed origins.

After implementing these changes, you should be able to make API requests from Next.js to WordPress without encountering the CORS error.

