function getWallpaper() {
	// use the window.clipboardapi module to access the clipboard data
	const Clipboard = require('clipboardy');
	const clipboardData = new Clipboard().readSync();

	// convert the base64 encoded string to a png format
	const dataUrl = `data:image/png;base64,${clipboardData}`;

	// set the wallpaper variable in the style tag
	document.head.innerHTML += `<style type="text/css"> body { background-image: url('${dataUrl}'); } </style>`;
}
```

note that this implementation assumes you have installed the node.js package manager and the `clipboardy` module. This script uses `clipboardy` to read the current desktop wallpaper from the windows clipboard (using `window.clipboardapi` would be preferred but it's not cross-platform). Then it sets the `wallpaper` variable as the background image of the body element.

this solution may not work perfectly on all browsers since some browsers do not allow accessing the clipboard. You can try modifying the script to use other methods such as `navigator.mediadevicerecorder` or `localstorage`.

