Using the provided MATLAB code and the given latitudes and longitudes, we can calculate the distance between San Francisco and London on the Earth's surface as follows:

```matlab
% Define Earth's parameters
a = 6378137; % Earth's semi-major axis in meters
b = 6356752.3142; % Earth's semi-minor axis in meters
f = (a-b)/a; % Earth's flattening
e2 = 2*f - f^2; % Earth's eccentricity squared

% Convert latitudes and longitudes from degrees to radians
lat1 = deg2rad(37.7749);
lon1 = deg2rad(-122.4194);
lat2 = deg2rad(51.5074);
lon2 = deg2rad(-0.1278);

% Calculate heights above Ellipsoid for both points (set to zero here)
h1 = 0;
h2 = 0;

% Calculate radii of curvature in the prime vertical at each point
N1 = a/sqrt(1-e2*sin(lat1)^2);
N2 = a/sqrt(1-e2*sin(lat2)^2);

% Calculate Cartesian coordinates of both points
x1 = (N1+h1)*cos(lat1)*cos(lon1);
y1 = (N1+h1)*cos(lat1)*sin(lon1);
z1 = ((1-e2)*N1+h1)*sin(lat1);
x2 = (N2+h2)*cos(lat2)*cos(lon2);
y2 = (N2+h2)*cos(lat2)*sin(lon2);
z2 = ((1-e2)*N2+h2)*sin(lat2);

% Calculate distance between the two points in meters
d = sqrt((x1-x2)^2 + (y1-y2)^2 + (z1-z2)^2);

disp(d);
```

Running this code will output the calculated distance between San Francisco and London

SQL is primarily used for data retrieval and manipulation, rather than creating interactive reminders or notifications. However, you could use SQL to trigger a job or stored procedure that sends an external notification (such as via email, SMS, or desktop pop-up) when it's time for a break.

To accomplish this, you'll need to set up a job scheduling system outside of your SQL server, such as Windows Task Scheduler or a third-party tool like Cron (for Linux/UNIX systems). The job would call a stored procedure in your SQL database at regular intervals, which would calculate the elapsed time since the start of the current work session and send a notification if it exceeds 10 minutes.

Here's an example of what the stored procedure might look like:

```sql
CREATE PROCEDURE [dbo].[CheckBreakTimer]
AS
BEGIN
    DECLARE @elapsedTime INT;
    DECLARE @userId INT;
    DECLARE @startTime DATETIME2(3);
    DECLARE @breakDuration INTEGER = 600; -- 10 minutes in seconds

    SET NOCOUNT ON;

    SELECT TOP 1 @userId = userID, @startTime = start_time FROM work_sessions ORDER BY end_time DESC;

    IF @userId IS NULL OR @startTime IS NULL BEGIN
        RETURN;
    END

    DECLARE @currentTime DATETIME2(3) = SYSDATETIME();
    DECLARE @elapsedSeconds INT = DATEDIFF(second, @startTime, @currentTime);

    IF @elapsedSeconds >= @breakDuration BEGIN
        DECLARE @message NVARCHAR(MAX) = 'It's time for a break!';

        -- Send external notification here...

        RETURN;
    END

    RETURN;
END
```

